-- Backfill 112 verified verbatim excerpts into the herb citations.
--
-- Closes, for 112 rows, the structural gap that let fifteen fabricated or
-- unsupportable citations reach production: a citation with no quoted text
-- cannot be checked by anyone.
--
-- ── HOW THESE WERE ESTABLISHED ──────────────────────────────────────────────
-- All 201 excerpt-less citations were audited at their named sources, which
-- recovered a real passage for 126. Those 126 were then re-read by SIXTEEN
-- INDEPENDENT VERIFIERS who did not produce them, each in an isolated working
-- directory so no agent could confirm a sibling's downloaded file.
--
-- 124 came back (2 were not returned and are NOT loaded here). Of those:
--   112  VERBATIM_CONFIRMED        <- loaded by this migration
--     7  WRONG_SPECIES_OR_PART
--     3  NOT_IN_SOURCE
--     2  LOCATOR_WRONG
-- Only the 112 are written. The other 12 are documented and left alone: they
-- need corrected citations, not a backfill, and writing corrections without a
-- further verification pass is the failure this project keeps paying for.
--
-- ── THE FINDING THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE PASS RATE ────────────────────────
-- Of the 112 confirmed excerpts, 65 (58%) do NOT support a temperature, and
-- 50 of those cite King's American Dispensatory.
--
-- That is not a quotation problem. King's is an Eclectic materia medica: it
-- classifies by ACTION (tonic, nervine, astringent, relaxant) and assigns no
-- thermal quality anywhere. Citing it for a temperature is a category error
-- however perfect the quotation. The same holds for Cook, Felter, Ellingwood.
--
-- Loading the excerpt is still right, because it makes the citation CHECKABLE
-- and shows plainly what the source does and does not say. It does NOT make
-- those rows' temperature sourced, and nothing here should be read as claiming
-- it does. That is separate work.
--
-- ── SAFETY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Uses jsonb || to MERGE excerpt and locator into the existing object, so
-- title, author, year and url are preserved. No row whose citation was
-- withdrawn in 20260721170000 or 20260722100000 appears here; checked
-- explicitly before generating.
--
-- Idempotent: re-running merges the same keys with the same values.

begin;

update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Aindri (t), Rishabhi (u), Atirasa (v), Rishyaprokta (w), Payasya (x), Afwagandha (y), Sthira (z), Rohini (a), Vala (b), and Ativala (c), — these ten are Valya (invigorating).13   [footnote:] y. Withania somnifera ; Syn. Physalis flexuosa.", "locator": "Charaka Samhita, Kaviratna trans., Sutrasthana, chapter IV (the fifty decads of drugs, ''These are the fifty Astringents par excellence''), printed page 32; identifying footnote (y) on the same page. Verified in the archive.org OCR of the Kaviratna translation, item BIUSante_47357, file BIUSante_47357_djvu.txt."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H001';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "It helps to expectorate tough phlegm, and is effectual in all cold griefs or diseases of the chests or lungs, being taken either in syrup or licking medicine. [...] Dioscorides saith, that Hyssop boiled with rue and honey, and drank, helps those that are troubled with coughs, shortness of breath, wheezing and rheumatic distillation upon the lungs.", "locator": "Culpeper, The English Physician / Complete Herbal (1653), entry \"HYSSOP\", section ''Government and virtues''. Project Gutenberg ebook 49513, plain-text file 49513-0.txt."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H122';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "[King''s American Dispensatory 1898, Anethi Fructus.—Dill-Fruit:] The fruit has a strongly aromatic odor and taste. […] Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Carminative and stomachic, and used in the preparation of dill-water. The natives of India use the fruit largely in medicine and cookery. Flatulent colic and singultus, when due to disordered digestion, are relieved by the administration of dill-water or the oil of dill; the former in 1 or 2-drachm doses, the latter in from 2 to 5-drop doses on sugar. It possesses no advantages over the other aromatic seeds.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry headed ''Anethi Fructus.—Dill-Fruit.'' (Anethum graveolens; common names Dill seeds, Dill fruits); sections ''Description.'' and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.'' — https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/anethum_fructus.html . Companion entries at the same site: ''Aqua Anethi.—Dill Water.'' (anethum_aqua.html) and ''Oleum Anethi.—Oil of Dill.''"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H146';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，平。主邪氣腹痛，除血痹，破堅積、寒熱、疝瘕，止痛，利小便，益氣", "locator": "Shennong Ben Cao Jing, Sun Xingyan / Sun Fengyi recension (1799), section ==芍藥==, 草部中品. Raw wikitext: zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw. Cross-checked against Ben Cao Gang Mu (1596), Siku recension, 卷14 草部・芳草類, 芍藥, 氣味 section, which prints 「氣味苦平無毒」 and attributes the identical 主治 string to 本經."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H173';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"Of horizontal growth, cylindrical, 5 to 7 Cm. (2 to 3 inches) long; about 1 Cm. (2/5 inch) thick; rather sharply tuberculated, longitudinally wrinkled, dark-brown; fracture short, pale reddish-brown; bark thin; wood-wedges yellowish, small, forming a circle near the cambium line; medullary rays broad; central pith large; roots thin, fragile; inodorous; taste strongly astringent\" (U. S. P.). ||| \"Geranium is a powerful astringent. Used in infusion with milk in the second stage of dysentery, diarrhoea, and cholera infantum. In bowel disorders it is the chronic or subacute states in which it is applicable, and especially where the discharges are abundant and debilitating. The relaxation of membranes following the inflammatory stage is an indication for its use. […] From its freedom from any nauseous or unpleasant qualities, it is well adapted to infants and persons with fastidious stomachs.\" ||| Specific Indications and Uses.—\"Relaxed mucous tissues, with profuse, debilitating discharges; chronic diarrhoea, with mucous discharges; chronic dysentery; diarrhoea, with constant desire to evacuate the bowels; passive hemorrhages.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Geranium (U.S.P.)—Geranium\" (Geranium maculatum, Nat. Ord. Geraniaceae), sections \"History and Description\" (the U.S.P. description), \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" (opening sentences and the fastidious-stomachs sentence), and \"Specific Indications and Uses\" (complete). henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/geranium.html — note the slug is geranium.html; the citation''s stored url is the section root only."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H202';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "[Culpeper 1653, catalogue of simples, FRUITS.] _Temperate in respect of heat._ Raisins of the sun, Currants, Figs, Pine Nuts, Dates, Sebastens.\n\n[Culpeper 1653, FRUITS AND THEIR BUDS.] _Green Figs_, are held to be of ill juice, but the best is, we are not much troubled with them in _England_; dry figs help coughs, cleanse the breast, and help infirmities of the lungs, shortness of wind, they loose the belly, purge the reins, help inflammations of the liver and spleen; outwardly they dissolve swellings.\n\n[Culpeper 1653, THE FIG-TREE] The milk that issues out from the leaves or branches where they are broken off, being dropped upon warts, takes them away. ... A syrup made of the leaves, or green fruit, is excellently good for coughs, hoarseness, or shortness of breath, and all diseases of the breast and lungs", "locator": "Culpeper, Complete Herbal (Gutenberg ebook 49513): ''THE FIG-TREE'' entry; and appended ENGLISH PHYSICIAN AND FAMILY DISPENSATORY, catalogue of simples, ''FRUITS AND THEIR BUDS.'' section and the ''FRUITS.'' degree lists. Grieve: botanical.com/botanical/mgmh/f/figcom12.html, ''---Medicinal Action and Uses---''."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H230';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "合歡\\n味甘，平。主安五臟，利心志（《藝文類聚》作和心志，《御覽》作和心氣）。令人獻樂無憂。……生山谷。\\n《名醫》曰：生益州。\\n\\n[EMENDATION FLAGGED, NOT SILENTLY CORRECTED: the wikisource transcription reads 令人獻樂無憂. 獻 is an error for 歡. Cross-checked against Bencao Gangmu 卷35下, which quotes the same 本經 line as 令人歡樂無憂. I have left the corrupt character in the excerpt above and record the correct reading here.]\\n\\n[Stronger, UNCITED support for the same row, Bencao Gangmu (1596) 卷35下, 合歡, 木皮:] 木皮（去粗皮炒用）氣味甘平無毒。主治安五臟，和心志，令人歡樂無憂……（本經）煎膏消癰腫，續筋骨（大明）……折傷疼痛，研末酒服二錢匕（宗奭）。和血消腫止痛（時珍）。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing (Sun Xingyan 1799), 木部中品, entry 合歡 — zh.wikisource.org raw wikitext of 神農本草經_(孫星衍), line 2098 of the raw text, between 衛矛 and 白馬莖, closing the 上木，中品一十七種 block. Secondary: Bencao Gangmu (四庫全書本) 卷35下, 木部 喬木類, 合歡 (labelled 本經中品), sub-heading 木皮."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H256';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "厚朴　味苦，溫。主中風、傷寒、頭痛、寒熱，驚悸氣，血痹死肌，去三虫。 ｜ 《吳普》曰：厚朴，神農、岐伯、雷公：苦，無毒；李氏：小溫。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯), 卷二·中經, 木部中品, 厚朴 entry, including the embedded 吳普本草 quotation."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H278';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Its epidermis contains no astringency and should, therefore, be removed. The bark thus prepared is of a pale-brownish color, faintly odorous, very astringent, with a slight bitterness, tough, breaking with a stringy or fibrous fracture, and not readily powdered. […] Oak bark is slightly tonic, powerfully astringent, and antiseptic. It is useful, internally in chronic diarrhoea, chronic mucous discharges, passive hemorrhages, and wherever an internal astringent is required. […] Specific Indications and Uses. — Relaxation of mucous membranes, with unhealthy discharge; ulcerations, with spongy granulations.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Quercus Alba (U.S.P.) — White Oak'', sections ''History and Description'', ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'', and ''Specific Indications and Uses''. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/quercus.html (fetched in full via curl, 28,410 bytes, not truncated)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H101';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage. —Aromatic, stimulant, and irritant. Used to allay vomiting and sickness at stomach, to stimulate the digestive functions, and to improve the flavor or operation of other remedies, and prevent a tendency to their producing sickness or griping. Dose, from 6 to 10 grains. || Description. —\"...Cloves emit oil when scratched, and have a strong, aromatic odor, and a pungent, spicy taste\"—(U S. P.).", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), entry \"Caryophyllus (U. S. P.)—Cloves\", sections ''Description'' and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/syzygium.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H124';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Caraway is an aromatic carminative, used in flatulent colic, especially of children, and to improve the flavor of several medicinal compounds. The oil (Oleum Carui) is more generally used. The seeds are frequently added to cakes and confectionaries, to render them more agreeable, while, at the same time, they gently excite the digestive powers.\n\n[Taste, from the same entry:] \"Oblong, laterally compressed, about 4 or 5 Mm. (1/6 to 1/5 inch) long, usually separated into the 2 mericarps, which are curved, narrower at both ends, brown, with 5 yellowish, filiform ribs, and with 6 oil tubes. Caraway has an agreeable odor, and a sweetish, spicy taste\"—(U. S. P.).\n\n[Temperature, from the cross-linked sibling entry Oleum Cari:] Oil of caraway is a warm diffusible stimulant and carminative. It is frequently used to allay the griping and sickening effects of medicines, also to increase their flavor. Like oil of anise it allays flatulent colic, and is a local anodyne.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898 (Felter & Lloyd), entry \"Carum (U. S. P.)—Caraway\", sections \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" and \"Description and Chemical Composition\" — https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/carum.html . The word \"warm\" occurs only in the separate cross-linked entry \"Oleum Cari (U. S. P.)—Oil of Caraway\", section \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" — https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/carum_oleu.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H149';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"…roots thin, wrinkled, very fragile; inodorous; taste bitter and feebly acrid\"—(U. S. P.). ||| \"Physiologically, leptandra acts upon the gastric, hepatic, and intestinal apparatus. The fresh root is actively and dangerously cathartic, and has produced violent emesis and bloody purging, accompanied by vertigo, and administered to the pregnant female has produced miscarriage. In this state, it is totally unfit for a cathartic, but upon drying the root loses its drastic qualities, and becomes a safe cholagogue, laxative, and cathartic. In ordinary doses it does not produce copious alvine discharges, but gently stimulates the functions of the liver.\" ||| \"It is an exceedingly useful drug for conditions depending upon hepatic torpor. Small doses restore the liver to its normal condition. […] It relieves the constipated upper bowel, increases the biliary secretions, and acts as an unirritating intestinal tonic…\" ||| \"The root, which should be well dried and at least one year old after collecting, yields about 6 per cent of resin.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Leptandra (U.S.P.)—Leptandra\" (Veronica virginica L. / Leptandra virginica Nutt., Nat. Ord. Scrophularineae), sections \"Description\" (U.S.P. taste line), \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" (opening paragraph and the hepatic-torpor paragraph), and the LEPTANDRIN subsection (the one-year ageing line). henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/leptandra.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H203';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "三七〔綱目〕\n釋名　山漆〔綱目〕、金不换\n根氣味　甘微苦温無毒\n主治　止血散血定痛　金刃箭傷跌撲杖瘡血出不止者嚼爛塗或為末摻之其血即止　亦主吐血衂血下血血痢崩中經水不止産後惡血不下血運血痛赤目癰腫虎咬蛇傷諸病〔時珍〕", "locator": "本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷十二下, 草部・山草類下, 三七 entry (釋名: 山漆、金不換). Retrieved as raw wikitext from zh.wikisource.org — note the trap that bare 卷12 is a table of contents only; the substantive text is at 卷12下."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H231';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "蘇（别錄中品）　　校正：自菜部移入此。\\n釋名：紫蘇（食療）、赤蘇（肘後方）、桂荏。時珍曰：蘓從穌，音酥，舒暢也。蘓性舒暢，行氣和血，故謂之蘇。\\n莖葉氣味：辛，温，無毒。（李廷飛曰：不可同鯉魚食，生毒瘡。）\\n主治：下氣，除寒中，其子尤良（别録）。除寒熱，治一切冷氣（孟詵）。補中益氣，治心腹脹滿，止霍亂轉筋，開胃下食，止脚氣，通大小膓（日華）。……解肌發表，散風寒，行氣寛中，消痰利肺，和血温中止痛，定喘安胎，解魚蟹毒，治蛇犬傷（時珍）。\\n子氣味：辛，温，無毒。主治：下氣，除寒温中（别錄）。……發明：時珍曰：蘓子與葉同功，發散風氣宜用葉，清利上下則宜用子也。", "locator": "Bencao Gangmu (四庫全書本) 卷14, 草部 芳草類, entry 蘇, sub-headings 釋名 / 集解 / 莖葉氣味 / 主治 / 發明, and the following 子氣味 block. zh.wikisource.org raw wikitext of 本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷14, lines 372-379."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H257';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"Ocynum. Basil, hot and moist. The best use that I know of it, is, it gives speedy deliverance to women in travail. Let them not take above half a dram of it at a time in powder, and be sure also the birth be ripe, else it causes abortion.\" ｜ \"Intemperate and hot in the first degree, are ... Bazil, Self-heal.\" ｜ from the monograph: \"And away to Dr. Reason went I, who told me it was an herb of Mars, and under the Scorpion ... I dare write no more of it.\"", "locator": "Culpeper, monograph headed \"GARDEN BAZIL, OR SWEET BAZIL\" (Descript. / Place / Time / Government and virtues); and the appended Galenical catalogue of simples, herbs, s.v. \"Ocynum\"; and the catalogue of herbs by degree, \"hot in the first degree\". Verified in TWO independent printings: archive.org mobot31753000789674 (1814) and archive.org b22009863 (1846), which agree verbatim on \"Ocynum. Basil, hot and moist.\""}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H279';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Aindri (t), Rishabhi (u), Atirasa (v), Rishyaprokta (w), Payasya (x), Afwagandha (y), Sthira (z), Rohini (a), Vala (b), and Ativala (c), — these ten are Valya (invigorating).13   [footnote:] v. Called also Satavari or Satamuli. Asparagus racemosus.", "locator": "Charaka Samhita, Kaviratna trans. (1890), Sutrasthana, chapter IV (the fifty decads), printed page 32, third item of the Valya decad (''Atirasa''); identifying footnote (v) on the same page. Verified in archive.org item BIUSante_47357, file BIUSante_47357_djvu.txt."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H103';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Senna is a certain, manageable, and convenient cathartic, very useful in all forms of febrile diseases in which a laxative action is desired... Its influence is chiefly exerted on the small intestines, augmenting their mucous secretions, exciting increased peristaltic motion, and producing loose, yellowish-brown evacuations. [...] It does not act as a sedative, as is the case with some other cathartics, nor as a refrigerant; but has a slight stimulating influence, insufficient, however, to contraindicate its use in case of general excitement or reaction. [...] Senna is contraindicated in an inflammatory condition of the alimentary canal, or even irritation of those parts, general debility, hemorrhoids, prolapsus ani, etc. [...] Constipation does not follow its employment.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), entry \"Senna (U. S. P.)—Senna\", sections ''Description'', ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'' and ''Specific Indications and Uses''. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/cassia.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H127';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage. —PARSLEY. Diuretic, relieving urinary irritation. Very useful in dropsy, especially that following scarlatina, and other exanthematous diseases. Also used in retention of urine, strangury, and gonorrhoea. Parsley seeds have a powerful odor, somewhat like that of turpentine, and a spicy, pungent taste, and have been used as a carminative, and for the same purpose as the root...\n\n[Taste of the root, from the History section:] The seeds, herb, and root, are the medicinal parts; the root has rather an agreeable odor, and a saccharine, slightly spicy taste, and should be used while fresh.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898 (Felter & Lloyd), entry ''Petroselinum.—Parsley'', sections ''History'' (root taste) and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'' (action, seed taste). https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/petroselinum.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H155';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味辛，溫。主胸中瘕熱逆氣，利水谷。… 一名橘皮。", "locator": "Shennong Ben Cao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), section ==橘柚==, filed 木部上品 with the editorial note 「舊在果部，非」 (formerly in the fruit section; incorrect). Raw wikitext: zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw. Cross-checked against Ben Cao Gang Mu (1596), Siku recension, 卷30 果部, 橘 → 黃橘皮, which prints 「氣味苦辛溫無毒　主治胸中瘕熱逆氣利水穀…{本經}」."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H175';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"…pappus consisting of 2 or 3 awns of the length of the disk-florets; odor balsamic; taste pungently aromatic and bitter\"—(U. S. P.). ||| \"The grindelias leave in the mouth a bitter, acrid sensation, which persists for some time and is accompanied or followed by an increased flow of saliva. On account of their irritant effects upon the kidneys, they act as diuretics. The brain and cord are first stimulated by them, followed by motor impairment of the lower extremities and a desire to sleep. The number of respirations are reduced by them.\" ||| \"Grindelia robusta has been found especially efficient in asthma, giving prompt relief, and effecting cures in cases previously rebellious to medication. […] It has likewise been found efficient in bronchial affections, in pertussis, and in some renal maladies.\" ||| Specific Indications and Uses.—GRINDELIA ROBUSTA: \"Asthmatic breathing, with soreness and raw feeling in the chest; cough, harsh and dry; breathing labored, with a dusky coloration of the face in plethoric individuals. Locally, old atonic ulcers; full tissues; rhus poisoning.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Grindelia (U.S.P.)—Grindelia\" (Grindelia robusta Nutt. and G. squarrosa Dunal, Nat. Ord. Compositae), sections \"Description\" (closing clause of the U.S.P. paragraph), \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" (opening paragraph and the G. robusta paragraph), and \"Specific Indications and Uses\". henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/grindelia.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H204';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "CULPEPER 1653 (pre-1900, supports the energetics): \"A Brotanum, mas, fœmina. Southernwood, male and female. It is hot and dry in the third degree, resists poison, kills worms; outwardly in plaisters, it dissolves cold swellings, and helps the bitings of venomous beasts, makes hair grow: take not above half a dram at a time in powder.\"  Confirmed by his degree-tables: \"In the third degree. Southernwood male and female...\" and \"Dry in the third degree. Southernwood, male and female...\", and his virtue-catalogues \"Provoke the terms. Southernwood, Garlick...\" and \"Resist poison. Southernwood, Wormwood, Garlick...\".  GRIEVE 1931 (indications only, post-1900): \"Tonic, emmenagogue, anthelmintic, antiseptic and deobstruent.\" / \"The chief use of Southernwood is as an emmenagogue. It is a good stimulant tonic and possesses some nervine principle. It is given in infusion of 1 OZ. of the herb to 1 pint of boiling water...\" / \"Considerable success has also attended its use as an anthelmintic, being chiefly used against the worms of children, teaspoonful doses of the powdered herb being given in treacle morning and evening.\"", "locator": "Culpeper: The Complete Herbal, appended catalogue of simples, section ''HERBS AND THEIR LEAVES'', entry ''A Brotanum, mas, fœmina'' (Project Gutenberg ebook 49513, line 16129); degree-tables at lines 18603 and 18645; virtue-catalogues at lines 18807 and 18817. Grieve: A Modern Herbal, ''Southernwood'', botanical.com/botanical/mgmh/s/sowood69.html, section ''---Medicinal Action and Uses---''."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H233';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The rhizome and roots of Spigelia marilandica, Linné. […] The part used is the rhizome and its roots. […] Description.—Pinkroot is composed of a number of delicate, crooked, corrugated fibers, of a dark-brown color externally, issuing from a short, dark-brown rhizome. Age impairs the virtues of pinkroot. […] The U. S. P. describes pinkroot as \"…the rhizome internally with a whitish wood and a pith which is usually dark-colored or decayed; odor somewhat aromatic, taste sweetish, bitter and pungent.\" […] Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Pinkroot is an active and certain vermifuge, especially among children. In large doses, it is very apt to purge, and produce various unpleasant symptoms, as increased action of the heart and arteries, dizziness, dilatation of the pupils, imperfect vision, and muscular spasms, often terminating in convulsions, together with various other indications of narcosis. One of its more frequent effects is spasmodic twitchings of the eyelids. These symptoms seldom happen when catharsis is produced, either by the drug alone, or exhibited in combination with a purgative. […] administering it twice a day for a few days, and then giving an active purgative.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Spigelia\", sub-sections History / Description / Chemical Composition / Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/spigelia.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H258';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "桔梗　味辛，微溫。主胸脅痛如刀刺，腹滿，腸鳴幽幽，驚恐悸氣。生山谷。 ｜ 《吳普》曰：桔梗……神農、醫和：苦，無毒；扁鵲、黃帝：咸；岐伯、雷公：甘，無毒；李氏：大寒。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯), 卷三·下經, 草部下品, 桔梗 entry. Section head reads: 下藥，一百二十五種，為左使……多毒，不可久服。"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H280';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "干地黃　味甘，寒。主折跌絕筋，傷中，逐血痹，填骨髓，長肌肉，作湯，除寒熱積聚，除痹，生者尤良。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍 recension, 1799), 卷一 上經, 草部上品, entry 干地黃. Retrieved as raw wikitext from https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw (242,119 bytes, full file, grepped locally)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H104';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "...inner surface yellowish to light brownish, becoming dark-brown by age; smooth or finely striate; fracture short, yellowish, in the inner layer of thick bark somewhat fibrous; inodorous; taste bitter\"—(U. S. P.). ... Further trial has substantially sustained these assertions... It does not, however, succeed in all cases, but acts best where a tonic to the intestines is required. As it tones the whole intestinal tract, it is valuable in doses of 10 drops, after meals, for that dyspeptic condition which depends most largely upon constipation, and is due to intestinal weakness.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Rhamnus Purshiana (U. S. P.) — Cascara Sagrada'', sections ''Description'' (quoting the U.S.P. description) and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/rhamnus-purs.html — note the working slug is ''rhamnus-purs'', not ''rhamnus-pursh''."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H129';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "It has a strong, peculiar, aromatic odor, and an aromatic, faintly bitter taste, followed by coolness in the mouth during inhalation. … \"odor aromatic; taste pungent\" (U. S. P.). … The carminative, antispasmodic, and stimulant properties of spearmint are somewhat inferior to those of peppermint; its principal employment is for its diuretic and febrifuge virtues. As a febrifuge, it is superior to peppermint, and may be used freely in warm infusion. … Specific Indications and Uses.—Scanty secretion of urine with frequent desire to urinate; simple nausea.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Mentha Viridis (U. S. P.).—Spearmint\", sections \"History and Chemical Composition\", \"Description\" (quoting U.S.P.), \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\", and \"Specific Indications and Uses\". https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/mentha-viri.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H156';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，寒。主熱氣目痛、[眥]傷泣出，明目，腸[澼]，腹痛下利，婦人陰中腫痛。… 一名王連。生川谷。", "locator": "Shennong Ben Cao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), section ==黃連==, 草部上品. Raw wikitext: zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw. Independently confirmed against Ben Cao Gang Mu (1596), Siku recension, 卷13 草部・山草類, 黃連, which gives 「氣味苦寒無毒」 and quotes 本經 as 「主治熱氣目痛眥傷泣出明目腸澼腹痛下痢婦人隂中腫痛」."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H176';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "It breaks with a fibrous, tough fracture, giving a narcotic, opium-like odor, and its taste, though slight at first, soon becomes acrid and bitter. […] Internally administered, it relieves pain, overcomes spasm, allays nervous excitability, and induces sleep. It is a favorite remedy in prolonged insomnia, particularly in the aged, and in those of an excessively nervous temperament. […] Beside the active principle, piscidia (piscidin), some resin, caoutchouc, wax and fat, starch (1.34 per cent), were present; tannin was not found.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), entry ''Piscidia.—Jamaica Dogwood.'', sections ''Description'' (taste), ''Chemical Composition'' (tannin), and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/piscidia.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H205';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "CULPEPER 1653 (pre-1900, supports the energetics): \"They are both of them hot and dry, especially the Summer kind, which is both sharp and quick in taste, expelling wind in the stomach and bowels, and is a present help for the rising of the mother procured by wind; provokes urine and women''s courses... It cures tough phlegm in the chest and lungs, and helps to expectorate it the more easily...\"  And from his catalogue of simples: \"Satureia. Savory. Summer savory is hot and dry in the third degree, Winter savory is not so hot, both of them expel wind.\"  GRIEVE 1931 (post-1900, indications only): \"Savory has aromatic and carminative properties, and though chiefly used as a culinary herb, it may be added to medicines for its aromatic and warming qualities. It was formerly deemed a sovereign remedy for the colic and a cure for flatulence, on this account, and was also considered a good expectorant.\"", "locator": "Culpeper: The Complete Herbal, chapter ''WINTER AND SUMMER SAVOURY'' (Gutenberg ebook 49513, lines 11588–11614; the book''s own index gives p.166); catalogue of simples, entry ''Satureia'' (line 17245); degree-tables listing ''summer and winter Savory'' hot in the third degree (line 18609) and dry in the third degree (line 18650). Grieve: A Modern Herbal, ''Savory, Summer'', botanical.com/botanical/mgmh/s/savsum24.html, section ''---Medicinal Action and Uses---''."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H234';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The root, leaves, and berries of Phytolacca decandra, Linné. […] The official parts of this plant are the root and berries. The root, which is more commonly employed, should be gathered in the latter weeks of autumn, cleansed from dirt and impurities, sliced transversely, and carefully dried. […] The young, green shoots, as they start in the spring and before the leaves have developed, are used as a table vegetable, being considered the best substitute for asparagus. They become cathartic as they advance to maturity. […] The berries steeped in gin have long been popular as a remedy for chronic rheumatism. […] II. PHYTOLACCAE RADIX (U. S. P.), Poke-root.—\"Large, conical, branched and fleshy; mostly in transverse or longitudinal slices, wrinkled, grayish, hard; fracture fibrous… inodorous; taste sweetish and acrid\"—(U. S. P.). […] Therapeutically, phytolacca is emetic, cathartic, narcotic, and alterative. […] In diseases of the glandular apparatus phytolacca and iris are our best drugs. Unlike iris, though, the former is best suited to hard, lymphatic enlargements. […] No other remedy equals phytolacca in acute mastitis. […] With these conditions it may be employed in tonsillitis, follicular pharyngitis, stomatitis, aphthae…", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Phytolacca\", sub-sections Official Parts / History / Description (II. Phytolaccae Radix) / Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage / Specific Indications and Uses. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/phytolacca.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H259';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "防風　味苦，溫，無毒。主大風、頭眩痛，惡風風邪，目盲無所見，風行周身，骨節疼痹，煩滿。一名銅芸。生川澤。 ｜ 《吳普》曰：防風……神農、黃帝、岐伯、桐君、雷公、扁鵲：甘，無毒，李氏：小寒。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯), 卷一·上經, 草部上品, 防風 entry, including the embedded 吳普本草 quotation."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H281';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Xanthoxylum americanum (Northern prickly ash) is in curved or quilled fragments, about 1 Mm. (1/25 inch) thick […] inodorous; taste bitterish, very pungent. […] The bark, when chewed, imparts an aromatic, sweetish taste, followed by bitterness and persistent acridity. Its sialagogue properties are remarkable, inducing a copious flow of saliva, together with a great quantity of mucus from the buccal glands. […] In the stomach it creates a sense of warmth, and the flow of both gastric and intestinal juices is augmented. […] Owing to its action on blood stasis, overcoming capillary engorgement, it has been found useful in determining the rash to the surface in the eruptive diseases.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Xanthoxylum (U.S.P.) — Xanthoxylum'', sections ''Description'' (BARK, quoting the U.S.P.) and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/xanthoxylum.html (fetched in full via curl, 37,620 bytes, not truncated)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H105';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "...externally grayish-brown, or orange-brown; internally showing a whitish and mealy, or somewhat horny, cortical layer, surrounding a circular wood-zone, the latter enclosing a broad pith; nearly inodorous; taste mucilaginous, bitterish, and acrid. The thick, woody, knotty rhizome, if present, should be removed\"—(U. S. P.). ... Sarsaparilla is generally considered as an alterative, though stated by some to possess diuretic, diaphoretic, and emetic properties.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Sarsaparilla'', sections ''Description'' (quoting the U.S.P.) and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/smilax.html. Species coverage confirmed in the same entry: ''...together with that coming from Central America (derived from Smilax ornata, Lemaire; see Pharm. Jour. Trans., 1889, p. 889), constitutes Jamaica sarsaparilla.''"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H132';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，平。主寒熱、鼠　，瘰　、癰腫，惡創，癭瘤，結熱蠱毒。一名異翹，一名蘭花，一名軹，一名三廉。生山谷。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷三·下經 (Book 3, Lower Class), entry 連翹. Retrieved from zh.wikisource.org raw wikitext, line 2869; lower-class section header at line 2416."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H177';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Charaka Samhita, Sutrasthana 4 (Kaviratna, 1892): \"Chandana, Tunga, Padmaka, Uçira, Madhuka, Manjishtha, Sariva, Payasya, Sita, and Lata,—these ten are Varnya or improve the complexion.\" … \"Haridra, Manjishtha, Suvaha, small cardamoms, Palindi, Chandana, Kataka, Girisha, Sindhuvara, and Cleshmataka,—these ten are Vishaghna (destroy poisons).\" … [the fever group, item (t) footnoted ''Rubia Cordifolia; syn. Rubia Munjishta''] \"—these ten cure fevers.\" ||| Bhavaprakasa Nighantu, Haritakyadi Varga (Srikantha Murthy translation): \"Manjistha is sweet bitter, astringent, bestows good voice and colour complexion, hard for digestion, hot in potency, mitigates ślesma (kapha), cures poison, dropsy, diseases of vagina, eyes and ears, diarrhoea with bleeding, leprosy (and other skin diseases), herpes, wound and diabetes.\"", "locator": "Charaka Samhita, Sutrasthana ch. 4, in Kaviratna''s English translation (Calcutta, D.C. Dass & Co., 1892): Varnya group at p. 33, Vishaghna group at p. 35, fever-curing (jvarahara) group at p. 43. archive.org id ZHNR_charaka-samhita-by-abhinash-chandra-english-pages-partly-damaged-missing-pages-1. ||| Bhavaprakasa Nighantu, Haritakyadi Varga, entry ''Manjistha'', at p. 190 of the Srikantha Murthy Sanskrit-and-English edition (Krishnadas Academy, Varanasi, 2001; MODERN TRANSLATION of a 16th-century text). archive.org id bhavaprakasa-vol-1-including-nighantu-by-bhavamisr."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H206';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "ON TASTE: \"Internally, it is soft, spongy, white or slightly straw-colored, the central portion being less firm than the outer. It is odorless, acrid to the taste, not bitter. Chamaelirium (Helonias), on the contrary, is very bitter.\"  ON ACTION: \"Enough is known, however, to place it among the simple bitter tonics and stomachics, and as such it is employed to promote the appetite and aid digestion, and in flatulence, colic, borborygmi, etc. This root and its preparations are almost entirely employed in dyspeptic conditions; while, in the abnormal conditions of the female reproductive organs, the chamaelirium is used.\"  ON SUBSTITUTION: \"The commercial drug, under this name, as found upon the market, is generally the rhizome of Chamaelirium... We can not recall a single instance where Aletris farinosa was adulterated with Chamaelirium and yet so universal has the substitution of the last become, that Prof. King, in describing the root of Aletris... has given a description of that of Chamaelirium...\"  ON THE PART USED: \"The rhizome of Aletris farinosa, Linné, gathered after the plant has flowered.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Aletris.—Aletris.'', sections ''History and Description'' and ''Action, Medical Uses and Dosage''; henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/aletris.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H236';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，寒。主疥瘙、痂癢、惡創，殺虫，留熱在骨節間，明目。一名青蒿，一名方潰。", "locator": "神農本草經（孫星衍輯本，1799）卷三·下經，草部下品，「草蒿」條. Retrieved zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw. Second work, verified separately: 葛洪《肘後備急方》卷三「治寒熱諸瘧方第十六」— 「又方 青蒿一握。以水二升漬，絞取汁。盡服之。」(zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=肘後備急方/卷3&action=raw)"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H263';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "牡丹\n味苦辛，寒。主寒熱，中風、螈　、痙，驚癇邪氣，除症堅，瘀血留舍腸胃，安五臟，療癰創。一名鹿韭，一名鼠姑。生山谷。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯本), 卷二·中經, 草部, entry 牡丹. Sun Xingyan recension, zh.wikisource.org raw wikitext (https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw), line 1862 of retrieved file; grade placement independently confirmed against the 中經 contents list at line 1386, where 牡丹 appears under 上草，中品四十九種."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H282';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The fruit is fleshy, roundish-oblong, berried, of a dark-purplish color, formed of the confluent, succulent scales, marked with 3 prominences, or vesicles at top, ripening the second year from the flower, and containing 3 bony, angular seeds […] Their odor is peculiar, terebinthine, and aromatic, and their taste terebinthine and sweetish, succeeded by some bitterness […] Both the berries and oil are stimulating, carminative, and diuretic. […] Pyelitis, pyelo-nephritis, and cystitis when chronic, and particularly when in old people, are relieved by juniper. […] It is very useful in the dropsy following scarlatina, and other infectious diseases.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Juniperus — Juniper'', sections ''Botanical Source'', ''History and Description'', and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/juniperus.html (fetched in full via curl, 19,299 bytes, not truncated)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H106';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Saw palmetto appears, from clinical reports, to be a nutritive tonic. ... It is said to enlarge wasted organs, as the breasts, ovaries, and testicles... We would rather regard it a remedy for prostatic irritation and relaxation of tissue than for a hypertrophied prostate. ... Specific Indications and Uses.—Relaxation of parts, with copious catarrhal discharges; lack of development, or wasting away of testicles, ovaries, or mammae; prostatic irritation, with painful micturition, and dribbling of urine, particularly in the aged.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Serenoa (Sabal) — Saw Palmetto'', sections ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'' and ''Specific Indications and Uses''. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/serenoa.html (the slug is ''serenoa''; ''sabal'' 404s)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H133';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "本草綱目 卷三十二 果之四, 茗 (即茶): 「葉氣味苦甘㣲寒無毒」 … 「主治瘻瘡利小便去痰熱止渴令人少睡有力悅志」(神農食經) … 「下氣消食作飲加茱茰葱薑良」(蘇恭) … 「破熱氣除瘴氣利大小腸」(藏器) … 「清頭目治中風昬憒多睡不醒」(好古) || GRIEVE (1931), for contrast: \"---Medicinal Action and Uses--- Stimulant, astringent. It exerts a decided influence over the nervous system, generally evinced by a feeling of comfort and exhilaration; it also causes unnatural wakefulness when taken in quantity.\"", "locator": "Bencao Gangmu (Li Shizhen, 1596), Siku Quanshu recension, juan 32 (卷32), section 果之四 味類, entry 茗 (headed 唐本草, 校正 自木部移入此), the 氣味 and 主治 headings. Raw wikitext: https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目 (四庫全書本)/卷32&action=raw . Grieve comparison: https://www.botanical.com/botanical/mgmh/t/tea---08.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H160';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，寒。主五內邪氣，胃中熱氣，面赤、酒泡、　鼻，白賴、赤癩，創瘍。一名木丹。生川谷。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷二·中經 (Book 2, Middle Class), entry 卮子 (editor''s note: 舊作梔, older texts write 梔). Retrieved from zh.wikisource.org raw wikitext, line 1978; middle-class section header at line 1384."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H178';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Partridgeberry is parturient, diuretic, and astringent. Used in dropsy, suppression of urine and diarrhoea, in decoction. It seems to have an especial affinity for the uterus, exerting a powerful tonic and alterative influence upon this organ, and has hence been found highly beneficial in many uterine derangements, as in amenorrhoea, some forms of dysmenorrhoea, menorrhagia, chronic congestion of the uterus, enfeebled uterine nervous system, etc. It is said that the squaws drink a decoction of this plant for several weeks previous to their confinement, for the purpose of rendering parturition safe and easy.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), entry ''Mitchella.—Partridgeberry.'', section ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/mitchella.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H208';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "ON ACTION AND INDICATIONS: \"In large doses, broom is emetic and cathartic; in small doses, diuretic. Used in all chronic forms of dropsy, said to never fail in increasing the flow of the urine; especially beneficial in dropsy of the thorax, combined with diseases of the lungs. Scurvy and jaundice have been successfully treated with it.\"  ON TASTE: \"All parts of the plant have a peculiar, nauseously amarous taste, and, when rubbed, have a characteristic odor.\" and, quoting the U.S.P.: \"odor peculiar when bruised; taste disagreeably bitter\".  ON TOXICITY: \"Broom is not without decided physiological effects—a staggering gait, impaired vision, and profuse vomiting and sweating have resulted from its use.\"  ON THE PART USED: \"The tops (Scoparii Cacumina, Br. Pharm.; Herba Scoparii) are the official parts. The seeds are also employed, and may be preserved for a longer time than the former.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Scoparius (U. S. P.)—Scoparius.'', sections ''History and Description'', ''Chemical Composition'' and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''; henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/cytisus-scop.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H237';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "澤瀉\n味甘，寒。主風寒濕痹，乳難。消水，養五臟，益氣力，肥健。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯本), 卷一·上經, 草部, entry 澤瀉 (line 402 of retrieved raw wikitext). Upper-grade placement independently confirmed against the 上經 contents list at line 36, where 澤瀉 appears in 草上品七十三種. The 卷一·上經 division begins at line 32."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H284';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"globular, about 4 Mm. (⅙ inch) in diameter, reticulately wrinkled, brownish-black or grayish-black, internally lighter, hollow, with an undeveloped embryo; odor aromatic; taste pungently spicy\"—(U. S. P.). ... Pain and redness are the results of the local application of powdered pepper. Internally administered it excites in the mouth and fauces a burning sensation, warms the stomach and slightly quickens the circulation. Abdominal heat and burning, marked thirst, vomiting, fever, and sometimes convulsions follow an excessive dose. ... Black pepper is a gastro-intestinal stimulant ... It has been advantageously used as a carminative to remove flatulency ... As a gastric stimulant it is very valuable in congestive chill, cholera morbus, and associated with hydrastis, nux vomica, or other stomachic bitters is effectual in atonic dyspepsia. ... Specific Indications and Uses.—Gastric atony; congestive chills.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry heading \"Piper (U. S. P.)—Piper.\", sections \"History and Description\" (U.S.P. description of the berry) and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" / \"Specific Indications and Uses\". Live text: https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/piper-nigr.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H107';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，平。主心腹，去腸胃中結氣，飲食積聚，寒熱邪氣，推陳致新。一名地熏。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍) — Sun Xingyan recension, 1799, 草部上品 (Herbs, Upper Grade), entry ==柴胡==. Retrieved as raw wikitext from zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw, line 329 of the raw text. Wu Pu''s gloss follows immediately in the same entry: 《吳普》曰：茈葫...神農、岐伯、雷公：苦，無毒."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H134';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "THE NUT, when dry, is subglobular, compressed, from 1 to 1 ½ inches in diameter… The internal portion is starchy, yellowish-white in color, and has an unpleasant, bitter taste. … Horse-chestnut bark is tonic, astringent, febrifuge, narcotic, and antiseptic. … Specific medication has taught us that it is a remedy, not for active conditions, but for congestion and engorgement. It is indicated in general by capillary engorgement—a condition of stasis—with vascular fullness and sense of soreness, throbbing, and malaise all over the body. … The pile-tumors are purple, large, do not bleed as a rule, but there is a sense of fullness, or spasm of the parts… Specific Indications and Uses.—Visceral neuralgia, due to congestion; soreness of the whole body, with vascular fullness, throbbing, and general malaise; … large, purple pile-tumors.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Hippocastanum.—Horse-Chestnut\" (the bark and fruit of Aesculus Hippocastanum, Linné), sections \"Description\", \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" and \"Specific Indications and Uses\". https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/aesculus-hipp.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H161';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味甘，平。主心腹結氣，傷中、傷飽，胃絡脈絕，羸瘦短氣。久服，輕身、不老、不飢。生川谷及堤阪。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷一·上經 (Book 1, Upper Class), entry 麥門冬. Retrieved from zh.wikisource.org raw wikitext, line 342; upper-class section header at line 32, upper-class name-list at line 36."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H181';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The leaves have no odor when dried, but when fresh and rubbed they are rather fragrant; their taste is astringent, sweetish, and not disagreeably bitter. […] Diuretic, tonic, alterative, and astringent. […] Specific Indications and Uses.—Atonic and debilitated states of the urinary organs, giving rise to lingering disorders, with scanty urine, but excessive voidings of mucus, muco-pus, or bloody muco-pus, offensive or non-offensive in character; smarting or burning pain with dysuria; chronic irritation of the urethra and prostate; chronic relaxation of the bladder walls; chronic prostatitis, with vesical catarrh.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), entry ''Chimaphila (U. S. P.)—Chimaphila.'', section ''History'' (the taste sentence), section ''Description and Chemical Composition'' (tannic acid, chimaphilin, arbutin), sections ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'' and ''Specific Indications and Uses''. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/chimaphila.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H209';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Sanskrit, in IAST transliteration as transmitted by GRETIL (Trikamji Varanasi edition, Chaukhambha 1994), Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 1.3.30–31:  \"maṇḍūkaparṇyāḥ svarasaḥ prayojyaḥ kṣīreṇa yaṣṭīmadhukasya cūrṇam / raso guḍūcyāstu samūlapuṣpyāḥ kalkaḥ prayojyaḥ khalu śaṅkhapuṣpyāḥ //\"  \"āyuḥpradānyāmayanāśanāni balāgnivarṇasvaravardhanāni / medhyāni caitāni rasāyanāni medhyā viśeṣeṇa ca śaṅkhapuṣpī //\"  Rendering of the operative clause: these rasāyanas are medhya (intellect-promoting), and Śaṅkhapuṣpī is medhya especially. Śaṅkhapuṣpī also appears three further times in this chapter as a compound ingredient only, at Ci.1.1.48, Ci.1.1.58 (the Brāhma rasāyana formula) and Ci.1.3.24.", "locator": "Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1 (Rasāyanādhyāya), pāda 3 (Karapracitīya), verses 30–31 — reference markers car_6,1.3.30 and car_6,1.3.31 in the GRETIL plain-text transformation of sa_agniveza-carakasaMhitA-parts-comm (source edition: J. Trikamji, Varanasi: Chaukhambha Sanskrit Sansthan, 1994, Kashi Sanskrit Series 228)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H238';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，寒。主消渴熱中，除邪氣，肢體浮腫，下水，補不足，益氣。", "locator": "神農本草經（孫星衍輯本，1799）卷二·中經，草部中品，「知母」條. Retrieved zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H266';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The whole plant is nearly odorless, but has a very bitter, somewhat aromatic taste.\n\nAction, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Buckbean, when recent, and given in large doses, usually causes emesis. It is, when dried, tonic and astringent, or purgative, according to the dose. It is valuable as a tonic where digestion and blood making are impaired, and particularly when there is an associated uterine disease or irregularity, or when following the use of quinine in malarial disorders (Scudder). ... It has been given in dyspepsia, intermittent and remittent fevers, with advantage; and has been highly recommended in chronic rheumatism, hepatalgia, dropsy, worms, and some cutaneous diseases, and as a tonic in scrofula, and various cachectic affections.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Menyanthes.—Buckbean'' (the leaves and rhizome of Menyanthes trifoliata, Linné; Nat. Ord. Gentianaceae). Taste sentence is in the ''History'' section; the remainder is the opening of ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. Retrieved in full from https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/menyanthes.html. Book navigation places it between ''Menthol (U.S.P.)'' and ''Methyl Salicylas (U.S.P.)''. The ellipsis in the excerpt elides one intervening sentence on tincture dosage; nothing is elided that changes the sense."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H287';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "KING''S 1898: \"...giving, when pulverized, a light brown powder, of a pungent, peculiar, spicy odor, a bitter taste succeeded by astringency, acridity, and a stinging sensation which gradually extends to the fauces, where it leaves an unpleasant feeling and a sense of constriction... Bayberry bark is astringent and stimulant, and as such is valuable in debilitated conditions of the mucous membranes... It was largely employed by the followers of Samuel Thomson, in catarrhal states of the alimentary tract... In larger doses (5 to 20 drops) it is a decided gastric stimulant... Specific Indications and Uses.—Profuse mucous flows; catarrhal states of the gastro-intestinal tract; atonic diarrhoea, typhoid dysentery, atony of the cutaneous circulation; full oppressed pulse. Locally and internally—sore mouth; spongy, flabby, bleeding gums... Bayberry bark was a constituent of ''Thomson''s Composition Powder or No. 6.''\" || THOMSON 1822: \"It is an exceiient [excellent] medicine either taken by itself or compounded with other articles; and is the best thing for canker of any article I have ever found. it is highly stimulating and very pungent, pricking the glands and causing the saliva and other juices to flow freely. Is good used as tooth-powder, cleanses the teeth and gums and removes the scurvy... It may be given to advanlaffe [advantage] in a relax and all disorders of the bowels.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry heading \"Myrica.—Bayberry.\", sections \"History and Description\" (BAYBERRY BARK paragraph), \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\", and \"Specific Indications and Uses\" — https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/myrica.html. || Thomson, New Guide to Health (1822 ed.), entry heading \"Bayberry; or Candleberry\", running pp. 223-224 (the printed folio \"224 New Guide to Health\" falls inside the entry) — archive.org identifier newguidetohealth00thom, OCR file newguidetohealth00thom_djvu.txt."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H108';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，寒。主五臟、腸胃中結熱，黃膽，腸痔，止泄利，女子漏下赤白，陰陽蝕創，一名檀桓。生山谷。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷一·上經 (Book 1, Upper Class), entry 檗木 (Bo Mu). Retrieved from zh.wikisource.org raw wikitext, line 1012. IMPORTANT: the wikisource section header at line 1010 reads literally ''==木=='' — the 檗 glyph is DROPPED from the transcription. Identity is confirmed by the entry''s own 案 note at line 1014: 《說文》云：檗，黃木也. I am flagging this emendation rather than silently normalising it."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H182';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Bhavaprakasa Nighantu, Haritakyadi Varga (Srikantha Murthy translation): \"Vidanga is pungent, penetrating, hot in potency, dry (causes dryness), increases digestive fire, easily digestable, cures colic, flatulence, [and] enlargement of the [abdomen], mitigates kapha and vata, removes worms and constipation.\" ||| Charaka Samhita, Sutrasthana 4 (Kaviratna, 1892): \"Akhira, Maricha, Gandira, Kevuka, Nirgunthi, Vidanga, Kinihi, Acwadangshtra, Vrishaparni, and Akhuparni,—these ten are Krimighna [anthelmintic].\" ||| Charaka Samhita, Sutrasthana 25 (Kaviratna, 1892): \"Of all things used as anthelmintics, the foremost are Vidanga (the seeds of Embelia Ribes; syn. E. glandulifera).\"", "locator": "Bhavaprakasa Nighantu, Haritakyadi Varga, entry ''Vidanga'', p. 177 of the Srikantha Murthy Sanskrit-and-English edition (Krishnadas Academy, Varanasi, 2001; MODERN TRANSLATION of a 16th-century text), archive.org id bhavaprakasa-vol-1-including-nighantu-by-bhavamisr. ||| Charaka Samhita Sutrasthana 4, Krimighna mahakashaya, at p. 35 of Kaviratna''s English translation (Calcutta, 1892). ||| Charaka Samhita Sutrasthana 25 (Yajjah-purushiya), ''foremost'' list, at p. 279 of the same. archive.org id ZHNR_charaka-samhita-by-abhinash-chandra-english-pages-partly-damaged-missing-pages-1."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H210';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "It has a persistent, alliaceous odor, and a bitter, alliaceous, acrid taste. [Description, quoting the U.S.P.]  ...  Its properties are stimulant, antispasmodic, expectorant, emmenagogue, and vermifuge (Ed.). Improper in inflammatory conditions of the system, but of marked value in purely functional nervous disorders, with excitability, and as a gastric stimulant in gastro-intestinal atony, with flatulence. [Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage]", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Asafoetida (U. S. P.)—Asafoetida\", sections \"Description\" and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\". Full text retrieved from https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/ferula-asaf.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H240';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，平。主風濕寒熱邪氣，熱結、黃膽。", "locator": "神農本草經（孫星衍輯本，1799）卷一·上經，草部上品，「茵陳」條. Retrieved zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H267';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"...translucent on the edges, sometimes marked with whitish veins; odor balsamic; taste aromatic, bitter and acrid\"—(U. S. P.). ... Myrrh is stimulant, especially to mucous tissues. It also exerts an antiseptic influence, and is used to promote expectoration, as well as menstruation. ... Large doses accelerate the pulse, augment the heat of the body, cause gastric heat and burning, great sweating and marked prostration... It is generally used in enfeebled conditions of the body, and has been found useful in cases of excessive mucous secretion... Its property of restraining the mucous discharges is observed to be most pronounced upon the renal and bronchial tract. ... It is contraindicated by arterial excitement or fever. ... Myrrh was formerly used as a dressing for indolent ulcers to promote granulation and alter the character of the discharges. ... Topically, it is a very useful application to indolent sores, gangrenous ulcers, and aphthous or sloughy sore throat, spongy or ulcerated conditions of the gums, caries of the teeth, etc.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry heading \"Myrrha (U. S. P.)—Myrrh.\", sections \"History and Description\" (U.S.P. description) and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\". The page is filed under the slug commiphora.html, not myrrha.html — https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/commiphora.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H109';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "सारिवायुगरं स्वादु रिनग्धं श॒क्रकरं गुड । अधिमान्द्ादचिश्वासकासामविषनाशनम्‌ । दोषनत्रयासप्रद्रञ्वरातीसारना शनम्‌ ॥ [OCR as printed] — emended reading: सारिवायुगलं स्वादु स्निग्धं शुक्रकरं गुरु । अग्निमान्द्यारुचिश्वासकासामविषनाशनम् । दोषत्रयासृक्प्रदरज्वरातीसारनाशनम् ॥ — AND, from King''s: \"It is brownish externally, has a feeble, bitter taste, and a peculiar aromatic odor, somewhat like that of sassafras, but which has been compared to that of new hay.\"", "locator": "(a) Bhavaprakasha Nighantu, गुडूच्यादिवर्गः (Guduchyadi varga), verse 218, under the heading अथ सारिवाद्वयस्य गुणानाह, p. 427 of the Chunekar/Chaukhambha edition — archive.org item ''bhavaprakash'', file bhavprakash-2_djvu.txt, character offset ~401,550. (b) King''s American Dispensatory (Felter & Lloyd, 1898), ''Hemidesmus.—Indian Sarsaparilla'', History/Description section — henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/hemidesmus.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H137';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage. — Willow bark is tonic, antiperiodic, and an astringent bitter. It has been given in intermittents, dyspepsia, connected with debility of the digestive organs, passive hemorrhages, chronic mucous discharges, in convalescence from acute diseases, and in worms. Although occasionally substituted for the cinchona bark, it is inferior in activity. In chronic diarrhoea and dysentery, the tonic and astringent combination of the willow renders it very eligible.\"  And from History and Description: \"The dried bark is met with more or less quilled, pliable and tough, with a faint odor, and a bitter taste, combined with some astringency.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, article \"Salix — Willow\" (the bark of Salix alba, Linné), section \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\"; Vol. II, p. 1702. Clean transcription at https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/salix-alba.html, cross-checked verbatim against the printed text (archive.org kingsamericandis02kinguoft_djvu.txt, lines 59485–59560)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H164';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味辛，平。主傷寒寒熱，心下堅，下氣，喉咽腫痛，頭眩胸脹，咳逆腸鳴，止汗。一名地文，一名水玉。生川谷。 [second passage, on toxicity and processing] 干薑　秦椒為使；惡黃連、黃芩、天鼠屎；殺半夏、莨菪毒。", "locator": "Passage 1: Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷三·下經 (Book 3, Lower Class), entry 半夏, wikitext line 2533; lower-class header at line 2416, lower-class name-list at 2419. Passage 2: same work, appendix 附：諸藥制使 (Drug Relations), entry 干薑 (dried ginger), wikitext line 3627."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H183';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The bark of the root is the medicinal part. It has a bitter, and somewhat unpleasant taste. […] \"nearly inodorous; taste sweetish, somewhat bitter and acrid\"—(U. S. P.). […] The bark is tonic, laxative, alterative, diuretic, and expectorant; the seeds are cathartic and emetic. In infusion, syrup, or extract, it has been successfully used in intermittents, dyspepsia, torpid liver, constipation, dropsy, and pulmonary affections. […] It stimulates the biliary flow, and has considerable anti-malarial influence […] Euonymin, as obtained from E. atropurpureus by Prof. Meyer and Dr. Romin, of Dorpat […] is a crystalline glucosid which corresponds in its physiological action closely with digitalin.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), entry ''Euonymus (U. S. P.)—Euonymus.'', sections ''History'' and ''Description'' (taste), ''Chemical Composition'' (the digitalin comparison), and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/euonymus.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H211';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "It has the unpleasant odor of the plant, and, when fresh, a persistent, acrid taste. ... Drying lessens the odor as well as the acridity of the plant, and age and exposure dissipate them entirely, consequently the root should be renewed annually. ... In medicinal doses it is a stimulant, exerting expectorant, powerful antispasmodic, and faintly narcotic influences. ... It has been successfully used in asthma, whooping-cough, nervous irritability, hysteria, epilepsy, and convulsions during pregnancy and labor; likewise in chronic catarrh, pulmonary, and bronchial affections.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Dracontium.—Skunk-Cabbage\" (Symplocarpus foetidus), sections \"History\", \"Description and Chemical Composition\", and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\". Full text from https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/dracontium.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H244';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "遠志\n\n味苦，溫。主咳逆傷中，補不足，除邪氣，利九竅，益智慧，耳目聰明，不忘，強志倍力。久服，輕身、不老。葉名小草，一名棘菀，一名　繞，一名細草。生川谷。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷一 草上品, entry 遠志. zh.wikisource.org 神農本草經_(孫星衍) raw wikitext, line 410. Upper-grade classification independently confirmed from the 草上品 roster at line 36 (''…薏苡仁　澤瀉　遠志　龍膽…''), and the class definition at line 34: 上藥一百二十種，為君…無毒。多服、久服不傷人。"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H268';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"About 4 or 5 Mm. (⅙ to ⅕ inch) long, oblong-ovate, flattened, obliquely pointed at one end, brown, glossy, covered with a transparent, mucilaginous epithelium, which swells considerably in water; the embryo whitish or pale greenish, with 2 large, oily, plano-convex cotyledons, and a thin perisperm; inodorous; taste mucilaginous, oily, and bitter.\"—(U. S. P.) ... \"Flaxseed is used as a demulcent and emollient. An infusion of the entire seeds, an ounce to a quart of water at 100° C. (212° F.), forms a mucilaginous draught which is much employed in ardor urinae and urinary diseases, nephritic pains, coughs, colds, colo-rectitis, pulmonary, gastro-enteric, and urinary inflammations. ... An infusion of flaxseed, or of flaxseed meal, forms an excellent laxative injection; and the meal added to boiling water, and made of the proper consistence, makes an excellent cataplasm.\" ... \"When the unbruised seeds are covered with water, a viscid, odorless, and almost tasteless mucilage is obtained, precipitable by alcohol.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry heading \"Linum (U. S. P.)—Linseed.\", sections \"Description\" (U.S.P. description), \"Chemical Composition\" (mucilage sentence) and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" — https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/linum.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H113';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "सख नवो ब्रंहणो ब्प्यः पुराणस्स्वतिरेखनः ॥ ४२ ॥ स्निग्धः काञ्जनसकाशः पक्ठजम्बूफरोपमः। नूतनो गुग्गुः भोक्त सुगन्धियंस्तु पिच्छः ॥ ४३ ॥ श्को दुगेग्धकभ्चैव स्यकतश्रृतिवणंकः । पुराणः स तु विज्ञेयो गुर्गुखरवीयं वर्जितः ॥ ४४ ॥ [OCR as printed] — emended: सर्वो नवो बृंहणो वृष्यः पुराणस्त्वतिरेखनः ॥४२॥ स्निग्धः काञ्चनसंकाशः पक्वजम्बूफलोपमः । नूतनो गुग्गुलुः प्रोक्तः सुगन्धिर्यस्तु पिच्छिलः ॥४३॥ शुष्को दुर्गन्धकश्चैव त्यक्तस्वकृतिवर्णकः । पुराणः स तु विज्ञेयो गुग्गुलुर्वीर्यवर्जितः ॥४४॥", "locator": "Bhavaprakasha Nighantu, कर्पूरादिवर्गः (Karpuradi varga), verses 42–44 (with the guna verses 38–41 immediately preceding), pp. 204–206 of the Chunekar/Chaukhambha edition — archive.org item ''bhavaprakash'', file bhavprakash-1_djvu.txt, character offset ~533,085–534,600."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H138';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage. — Of the physiological action of this agent little is known. To the taste it is bitter, and slightly aromatic. Large doses sometimes produce nausea and vomiting, and by some observers is said to produce contraction of the uterine muscular tissue. That it has a decided affinity for the female reproductive organs is well established. ... By its astringency it has proved of value in diarrhoea and dysentery. ... In the hyperaesthetic, or irritable condition of the uterus incident to highly nervous women, or as the result of overwork, it will be found an admirable agent. ... It is specifically indicated in cramp-like menstrual pains — pains decidedly expulsive and intermittent in character and in the various painful contractions of the pelvic muscles, so common to disorders of women. ... It acts promptly in spasmodic dysmenorrhoea, especially with excessive flow.\"  On the drug itself: \"It is fawn-colored externally, with a feeble odor, and a very bitter, slightly aromatic taste,\" and quoting the U.S.P., \"fracture short; inodorous, somewhat astringent, and bitter\" — (U.S.P.).", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, article \"Viburnum Prunifolium (U.S.P.) — Black Haw\", sections \"History and Description\" and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\"; Vol. II, p. 2060. Clean transcription at https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/viburnum-prun.html, cross-checked against the printed text (archive.org kingsamericandis02kinguoft_djvu.txt, lines 85653–85745)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H166';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味甘，平。主胸脅逆氣（《御覽》作疝氣），憂恚，驚邪，悸心下結痛，寒熱煩滿咳逆，口焦舌乾，利小便。", "locator": "神農本草經（孫星衍輯，1799），卷一·上經，木部上品，茯苓條 — the entry standing between 柏實 and 榆皮. Wikisource raw wikitext line 984 (https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw). Identity confirmed by the following 一名茯菟 / 茯神 alias note and the 《吳普》 gloss at line 986."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H184';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "苦參\n\n味苦，寒。主心腹結氣，症瘕積聚，黃膽，溺有餘瀝，逐水，除癰腫，補中明目，止淚。\n\n一名水槐，一名苦識。生山谷及田野。\n\n《名醫》曰：一名地槐，一名菟槐，一名驕槐，一名白莖，一名虎麻，一名芩莖，一名祿曰，一名陵郎。生汝南。三月、八月、十月采根，曝乾。", "locator": "神農本草經, Sun Xingyan (孫星衍) recension of 1799, 卷二 中經 (middle grade), 草部, entry 苦參. Retrieved as raw wikitext from https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw , entry heading at line 1540 of the raw file. Middle-grade placement independently confirmed against the recension''s own 中品 index at line 1386, where 苦參 appears in the list ''上草，中品四十九種''."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H214';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"Rhizome of horizontal growth, consisting of joints, 5 to 10 Cm. (2 to 4 inches) long... odor slight; taste acrid and nauseous\"—(U. S. P.). ... Therapeutically, this agent is alterative and cholagogue. It is one of our best agents to influence the process of waste and repair. It exerts a powerful catalytic action upon the lymphatic glandular system, and the ductless glands, as well as upon the liver, pancreas, and kidneys. ... Upon the gastro-intestinal tract it acts violently, causing acid vomiting, frequent, hydragogue catharsis, with intestinal burning and severe colic.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Iris (U. S. P.)—Iris\" (Iris versicolor, rhizome), sections \"Description\" (quoting the U.S.P.), \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\", and \"Specific Indications and Uses\". Full text from https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/iris-vers.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H245';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "署豫（舊作薯蕷，《御覽》作署豫，是）\n\n味甘，溫。主傷中，補虛羸，除寒熱邪氣，補中，益氣力，長肌肉。久服，耳目聰明，輕身、不飢、延年。一名山芋，生山谷。\n\n《吳普》曰：薯蕷……神農：甘，小溫；桐君、雷公：甘，無毒。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷一 草上品, entry 署豫 (= 薯蕷 / Shan Yao). zh.wikisource.org 神農本草經_(孫星衍) raw wikitext, lines 380-384. Upper-grade listing confirmed in the 草上品 roster at line 36."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H269';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Rhatany is a powerful astringent, with some slight tonic virtues. Constipation with slight dyspeptic symptoms may be induced by immoderate doses. It maybe employed internally with advantage in menorrhagia, hematemesis, passive hemorrhages, chronic diarrhoea, leucorrhoea, chronic mucous discharges, colliquative perspiration, and incontinence of urine.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), Krameria (U. S. P.)—Krameria, ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.'' paragraph. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/krameria.html — TRANSCRIPTION ARTIFACT: the source page reads ''It maybe employed''; ''may be'' is plainly intended. Quoted as-found rather than silently corrected. For part_used, same page, ''History and Description.'': \"The bark contains more of the medicinal virtues than the ligneous or woody part\", and the U. S. P. description \"bark … very astringent, inodorous … wood pale brownish-red, tough, with fine medullary rays, nearly tasteless.\""}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H293';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "It has a peculiar, rather agreeable, vinous, balsamic odor, and a very bitter, aromatic, somewhat acrid and persistent taste. ... Hoarhound is a stimulant tonic, expectorant, and diuretic. Its stimulant action upon the laryngeal and bronchial mucous membranes is pronounced, and it, undoubtedly, also influences the respiratory function. It is used in the form of syrup, in coughs, colds, chronic catarrh, asthma, and all pulmonary affections. ... the cold infusion is an excellent tonic in some forms of dyspepsia, acts as a vermifuge", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Marrubium (U. S. P.)—Marrubium\", sections \"History and Description\" (taste sentence) and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" (all remaining text). Live page: https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/marrubium.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H114';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "葛根　味甘，平。主消渴，身大熱，嘔吐，諸痹，起陰氣，解諸毒，葛谷，主下利十歲以上。一名雞齊根。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍 recension, 1799), 中品 · 草部, 葛根 entry — zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw, section heading ==葛根==. Independently corroborated at 本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷18上, 葛, 「葛根氣味甘辛平無毒」, which attributes the identical indication string 主治消渴身大熱嘔吐諸痺起隂氣解諸毒 to 本經."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H139';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"To the taste, bloodroot is harsh, bitter, acrid, and persistent, and, when swallowed, leaves an acridity and sense of constriction in the fauces and pharynx, and induces a feeling of warmth in the stomach. In small doses, it stimulates the digestive organs, and increases the action of the heart and arteries, acting as a stimulant and tonic; in larger doses it acts as a sedative to the heart, reducing the pulse, causing nausea, and, consequently, diaphoresis, increased expectoration, and gentle diuresis... If the dose be large, it provokes nausea, with violent emesis, vertigo, disordered vision, and great prostration.\"  On the expectorant action: \"It is important as a stimulating expectorant, to be used after active inflammation has been subdued. It may be employed in atonic conditions. It restores the bronchial secretions when scanty, and checks them when profuse... or when from atony the secretions are checked, it restores them, and removes the dry, harsh cough.\"  On the escharotic use: \"was also applied to exuberant excrescences for its escharotic action, and to ill-conditioned ulcers.\"  On the drug: \"a peculiar faintly virose odor, and a bitter, acrid, and pungent taste, leaving an impression in the fauces for some time after it has been chewed.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, article \"Sanguinaria (U.S.P.) — Sanguinaria\", sections \"History and Description\" and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\"; Vol. II, p. 1711 (the stimulating-expectorant passage is Felter, Ec. Med. Jour., quoted in the same section). Clean transcription at https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/sanguinaria.html, cross-checked against the printed text (archive.org kingsamericandis02kinguoft_djvu.txt, lines 60029–60420)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H167';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The bark of the root of Chionanthus virginicus, Linné. ... Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Chionanthus acts principally upon the abdominal glandular organs, and to some extent upon the venous system, relieving congestion. It is an alterative in the Eclectic meaning of that term. ... it is diuretic, tonic, and is said to be aperient and narcotic. It is exceedingly doubtful if the latter statement be true and its aperient property, if it possesses such, is the result of its cholagogue action. ... It is for its prompt and efficient action in hepatic derangements that we most value fringe-tree preparations. If there is any one thing true in specific medicine, it is that chionanthus has a decidedly specific action in jaundice. ... Hypertrophy of the liver, chronic hepatic inflammation, and portal congestion are speedily relieved by chionanthus. ... It never produces catharsis, but ptyalism has resulted from its use.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, ''Chionanthus.—Fringe-Tree.'', sections Botanical Source / History / Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/chionanthus.html (full page retrieved and read in full, not sampled)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H216';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "神農本草經 (孫星衍): 龍眼 — 味甘，平。主五臟邪氣，安志厭食。... 一名益智。生山谷。   ||   本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷三十一: 實氣味甘平無毒 {恭曰甘酸溫} 主治五臟邪氣安志厭食除蠱毒去三蟲... 開胃益脾補虛長智 {時珍}   ||   發明: 時珍曰食品以荔枝為貴而資益則龍眼為良蓋荔枝性熱而龍眼性和平也   ||   附方 歸脾湯: 治思慮過度勞傷心脾健忘怔忡虛煩不眠自汗驚悸", "locator": "(1) 神農本草經, 孫星衍 recension 1799, 中品 木部, 龍眼 條 — raw text from zh.wikisource.org, entry sits between 白棘 and 松羅. (2) 本草綱目 四庫全書本 卷三十一 果之三 夷果類, 龍眼 條 (marked 别録中品), the 實氣味 line, the 發明 paragraph, and the single 附方 (歸脾湯, credited to 濟生方)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H246';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "山茱萸\n\n味酸，平。主心下邪氣，寒熱，溫中，逐寒濕痹，去三虫。久服，輕身。一名蜀棗。生山谷。\n\n《吳普》曰：山茱萸……神農、黃帝、雷公、扁鵲：酸，無毒；岐伯：辛；一經：酸。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷二 木中品, entry 山茱萸. zh.wikisource.org 神農本草經_(孫星衍) raw wikitext, lines 2030-2034. Middle-grade (中品) placement confirmed from the 木中品 roster at line 1386."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H270';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "香蕈…氣味甘平無毒　主治益氣不飢治風破血", "locator": "本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷二十八, 菜部, 菜之五 芝栭類, entry 香蕈 (marked 日用, i.e. drawn from Wu Rui''s 日用本草, 1329). The 氣味/主治 line is attributed in the text to 吳瑞 (Wu Rui). Raw wikitext: https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷28&action=raw — offset ~26,930 of the raw page. Supporting 集解 (Wu Ying): 香蕈生深山爛楓木上小於菌而薄黃黒色味甚香美最為佳品 (''xiangxun grows on rotten maple wood deep in the mountains, smaller and thinner than jun, yellow-black, its flavour extremely fragrant and fine, the very best grade'')."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H294';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The odor is strongly aromatic, being intensified when the herb is bruised; and to the taste the plant is intensely bitter, with a persistently bitter aftertaste. ... It has been employed with success for the expulsion of the intestinal parasites—ascaris vermicularis and lumbricoides. ... In small doses it is a stimulant tonic, improves the appetite, and is useful in atonic states of the gastro-intestinal tract, as a tonic dyspepsia, especially when due to alcoholic excesses, in flatulent colic, and in obstinate diarrhoea. Large doses are apt to irritate the stomach and increase the action of the heart and arteries.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Absinthium (U. S. P.)—Absinthium\", sections \"Botanical Source and Description\" (taste/odor sentence) and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" (remaining text). Live page: https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/artemisia-absi.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H115';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "丹參　味苦，微寒。主心腹邪氣，腸鳴幽幽如走水，寒熱積聚；破症除瘕，止煩滿，益氣。一名卻蟬草。生川谷。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍 recension, 1799), 上品 · 草部, 丹參 entry — zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw, section heading ==丹參==. The attached 吳普 commentary in the same entry reads: 神農、桐君、黃帝、雷公、扁鵲：苦，無毒；李氏：小寒。岐伯：咸。"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H140';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味辛，溫。主中風入腦，頭痛，寒痹，筋攣緩急，金創，婦人血閉無子。生川谷。", "locator": "神農本草經（孫星衍輯，1799），卷一·上經，草部上品，芎藭條 — Wikisource heading renders as ==芎== at raw wikitext line 556, entry text at line 558. The entry immediately precedes ==蘼蕪== (line 568), which the text identifies as the leaf of the same plant (《名醫》曰：一名茳蘺，芎　苗也)."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H186';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "odor peculiar and unpleasant; taste bitter, acrid, and pungent\"—(U. S. P.).", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Stillingia (U. S. P.)—Stillingia.'', section ''History and Description'', quoting the U.S. Pharmacopoeia description of the root. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/stillingia.html. Second supporting passage, same entry, ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'': ''In less doses it is an alterative, exerting an influence over the secretory and lymphatic functions, which is unsurpassed by few, if any other of the known alteratives.''"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H220';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The taste is pleasantly spicy and balsamic, and the odor of the root peculiar and agreeably aromatic. The whole plant, when bruised, gives off this balsamic aroma. … Specific Indications and Uses.—Atonic states, with cough and irritation of the broncho-pulmonary tract; catarrhal affections.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), Aralia Racemosa.—Spikenard, ''Description and Chemical Composition.'' paragraph and the ''Specific Indications and Uses.'' line. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/aralia-race.html — For the Sweet taste and the alterative action, the cross-referenced entry: Aralia Nudicaulis.—False Sarsaparilla, ''History.'': \"the part employed is the root; when fresh it has an agreeable balsamic odor, and a pleasant, spicy, saccharine taste\", and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.'': \"Small spikenard possesses alterative properties, and is used in decoction or syrup as a substitute for sarsaparilla in all cases where an alterative is required. It is likewise used in pulmonary diseases.\" https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/aralia-nudi.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H295';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Black mustard-seed is \"about 1 Mm. (1/25 inch) in diameter, almost globular, with a circular hilum; testa blackish-brown or grayish-brown, finely pitted, hard; embryo oily, with a curved radical, and 2 cotyledons, one folded over the other; free from starch; inodorous when dry, but when triturated with water, of a pungent, penetrating, irritating odor; taste pungent and acrid\"—(U. S. P.). ... Mustard is an irritant, stimulant, rubefacient, vesicant, and diuretic. It is used in small quantities, internally, as a condiment and mild but efficient excitant of the organs of digestion. ... Externally, it quickly excites redness of the skin, and, if too long applied, inflammation, ulceration, and even sloughing", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, combined mustard entry (indexed under \"Sinapis Alba (U. S. P.)—White Mustard\"; the black-mustard sub-heading \"Sinapis Nigra (U. S. P.)—Black Mustard\" sits inside the same article). Taste sentence in \"Description\"; the rest in \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\". Live page: https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/sinapis.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H116';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "黃芩\n味苦，平。主諸熱黃膽，腸　泄利，逐水，下血閉，惡創，恆蝕火瘍。一名腐腸。生川谷。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan / Sun Fengyi recension (1799), 中品 (middle class), 草部, entry 黃芩. Retrieved as raw wikitext from zh.wikisource: https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw (heading ''==黃芩=='', immediately following 淫羊藿 and preceding 狗脊). Corroborating parallel: 本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷13, 黃芩, 根氣味 line — ''根氣味苦平無毒 {别録曰大寒…}'' and ''主治諸熱黃疸腸澼洩痢逐水下血閉惡瘡疽蝕火瘍{本經}''."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H142';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Shennong Bencao Jing (Sun Xingyan recension, 1799), 上品 (upper grade), entry headed 鞠華 (not 菊花): 「鞠華：味苦，平。主風，頭眩腫痛，目欲脫，淚出，皮膚死肌，惡風濕痹。」 — \"Ju hua: taste bitter, NEUTRAL. Governs wind; dizziness of the head with swelling and pain; the eyes feeling as though they would fall out; tearing; deadened flesh of the skin; aversion to wind, and damp impediment.\"  The entry continues 「久服，利血氣，輕身、耐老、延年。一名節華，生川澤及田野。」 — I am NOT offering that continuation as supporting evidence; see concerns.", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯, 1799), 卷一 草部上品, entry 鞠華 (Ju Hua), positioned between 菖蒲 and 人參. Raw wikitext at https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw, character offset ~10461.  Corroboration for the upper-grade claim and for the fuller taste range: 本草綱目 (1596), 卷十五 草之四 隰草類上, 菊, sections 氣味 and 集解 — https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷15&action=raw"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H169';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "味苦，溫。主風寒濕痹、死肌、痙、疸。止汗，除熱，消食，作煎餌。", "locator": "神農本草經（孫星衍輯，1799），卷一·上經，草部上品，==術== 條. Wikisource raw wikitext line 268; the philological 案 note is at line 274."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H187';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Collinsonia is said to be alterative, tonic, stimulant, and diuretic. It acts principally on the venous system and mucous tissues, and undoubtedly has a marked action on the vagus, relieving irritation in parts to which that nerve is distributed.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Collinsonia.—Stone-Root.'', section ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'', opening sentences. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/collinsonia.html. The passive-congestion claim: same section, ''In short, passive vascular engorgement with dilated capillaries, torpid portal circulation, and lack of muscular tonicity, call for stone-root. The keynote is a sense of weight and constriction in the part affected.'' Sensory description: section ''History'', ''The whole plant has a peculiar, lemon-like, balsamic odor, rather disagreeable in the root, and a spicy, pungent taste.''"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H221';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The dried rhizomae exhale a peculiar, characteristic, not unpleasant odor, more strongly developed when bruised, or rubbed between the fingers. The taste is very bitter, disagreeable and acrid.\n\nAction, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Helonias is tonic, diuretic, and vermifuge; in large doses, emetic, and, when fresh, sialagogue... It is more especially applicable in indigestion, dyspepsia, and mal-assimilation, where the trouble is reflex from, or associated with wrongs of the female reproductive apparatus... In diseases of the reproductive organs of females, and especially of the uterus, it is one of our most valuable agents, acting as a uterine tonic, and gradually removing abnormal conditions, while at the same time it imparts tone and vigor to the reproductive organs. Hence, it is much used in leucorrhoea, amenorrhoea, dysmenorrhoea, and to remove the tendency to repeated and successive miscarriages... In painful menstruation it has been found especially adapted to those cases in which there is pelvic fullness, a sensation as if the womb and rectum were distended with blood, and the aching, bearing-down organs feel as if they would fall out of the body.\n\nChamaelirin is said to act as a heart poison... The plant is said to kill cattle feeding on it; and the decoction to kill insects, bugs, and lice.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Chamaelirium (Helonias.)—Blazing Star'', Nat. Ord. Liliaceae; sections Description (taste), Chemical Composition (chamaelirin/heart poison), and Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/chamaelirium.html (24 KB page downloaded whole with curl and read in full)"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H250';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "杜仲\n\n味辛，平。主腰脊痛，補中，益精氣，堅筋骨，強志，除陰下癢濕，小便余瀝。久服，輕身、耐老。一名思仙。生山谷。", "locator": "Shennong Bencao Jing, Sun Xingyan recension (1799), 卷一 木上品, entry 杜仲. zh.wikisource.org 神農本草經_(孫星衍) raw wikitext, line 1058-1060. Upper-grade listing confirmed from the 木上品 roster at line 36 (''…桑上寄生　杜仲　女貞實…''); the non-toxic / long-term-use claim rests on the class definition at line 34: 上藥一百二十種，為君……無毒。多服、久服不傷人。"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H272';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"History.—This valuable medicinal plant is in the United States in damp soils, flowering in August and September. ... The leaves are exceedingly bitter, but inodorous, and communicate their properties to both water and alcohol.\"\n\n\"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Tonic, cathartic, and anthelmintic. Especially valuable in jaundice and hepatic diseases, likewise for the removal of worms, for which it may be used in powder or decoction, internally and also in injection. Used as a tonic in small doses, in dyspepsia, debility of the digestive organs, particularly when associated with hepatic inactivity, and during convalescence from febrile and inflammatory diseases.\"\n\n\"Specific Indications and Uses.—Gastro-intestinal debility, with hepatic torpor or jaundice; worms.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Chelone.—Balmony.\" (Chelone glabra, Linné; Nat. Ord. Scrophulariaceae), sections History; Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage; Specific Indications and Uses. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/chelone.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H297';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The whole plant has a strong, heavy, unpleasant smell, and a bitter, acrid, pungent taste, which is due to its volatile oil. ... Rue is emmenagogue, ecbolic, anthelmintic, and antispasmodic. In large doses it seems to be a narcotico-acrid poison. It is asserted to cause abortion, and such effect is accompanied with inflammation of the stomach and bowels, with cerebral disturbance. ... It has been successfully used in flatulent colic, hysteria, some nervous complaints, epilepsy, and as an excellent vermifuge.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Ruta.—Rue\", sections \"History, Description, and Chemical Composition\" (taste sentence) and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\" (remaining text). Live page: https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/ruta.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H117';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Buchu is an aromatic stimulant and tonic. It promotes the appetite, relieves nausea and flatulence, and acts as a diuretic and diaphoretic. In favoring the urinary secretion it augments both the solid and watery constituents. On the other hand, when the kidneys are excessively active, their action is restrained by buchu. It is principally used in chronic diseases of the urino-genital organs, as in cases of chronic inflammation of the mucous membrane of the bladder, irritable conditions of the urethra, in urinary discharges with increased deposit of uric acid, and in incontinence connected with diseased prostate. Profuse muco, or muco-puruluent discharges, with vesico-renal irritation, point to its use. […] Catarrh of the bladder, from the extension of gonorrhoea, from irritant injections, or due to gleet, is relieved by it. […] Under favorable circumstances a warm infusion of buchu leaves will cause diaphoresis.\n\n[on taste, from the Description section, quoting the U.S.P.:] \"…odor and taste strongly aromatic, somewhat mint-like, pungent and bitterish\"—(U. S. P.).", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry headed ''Buchu (U. S. P.)—Buchu.'' (botanical name Barosma betulina). Two sections: ''Description.'' (U.S.P. quotation, taste) and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.'' Full text at https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/barosma.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H143';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "至于子則甘平而潤性滋而補不能退熱止能補腎潤肺生精益氣此乃平補之藥所謂精不足者補之以味也", "locator": "Ben Cao Gang Mu (1596), Siku Quanshu recension, 卷36 木部・灌木類, entry 枸杞地骨皮, 發明 section, 時珍曰. Retrieved as raw wikitext: zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷36&action=raw. Reinforced in the same entry''s 氣味 note: 「子味甘氣平」 and, under 枸杞子, 「氣味苦寒（權曰甘平）… 滋腎潤肺榨油㸃燈明目」."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H170';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "It has a bitter, non-astringent taste, but no odor. When chewed it imparts a yellow color to the saliva. […] Berberis is a tonic and laxative. Formerly used extensively by practitioners in the New England States, in all cases where tonics are indicated, also in jaundice, and chronic diarrhea and dysentery.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Berberis.—Barberry'' (Berberis vulgaris, bark of the root and the berries), ''Description'' paragraph and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'' paragraph. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/berberis-vulg.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H188';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The sensible properties of the leaves of all the varieties (or species) of Turnera found upon the market, under the name damiana, are similar. All have a fragrant odor, resembling lemon-balm, and a pleasant, slightly aromatic taste.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Turnera.—Damiana.'', end of ''Botanical Source, History, and Description''. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/turnera.html. The actions passage, ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'': ''It will very likely be found to possess laxative, tonic, and diuretic properties only; and the aphrodisiac effects following its use, no more prove that these belong to it, than the same effects, that not unfrequently appear after the employment of many other agents prove that such agents possess similar excitant virtues.''"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H225';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The bark is rough, pealing off—each layer being of a different color, and which has probably given origin to the name \"seven barks.\"... The bark of the dried root has a rather pungent, aromatic, not disagreeable taste, somewhat similar to that of cascarilla bark.\n\nContrary to Baur''s statement, no tannin was found.\n\nThe power of curing or dissolving stone in the bladder is not claimed for it; it is only, while the deposits are small, when in that form of the disease known as gravel, that it is an efficient remedy... Thus it has chiefly an eliminatory action rather than any power to dissolve gravel. By its soothing action it relieves vesical and urethral irritation. Probably its greatest value lies in its power of preventing the formation of alkaline and phosphatic deposits... If taken in overdoses it will produce some unpleasant symptoms, as dizziness of the head, oppression of the chest, etc.\n\nSpecific Indications and Uses.—Vesical and urethral irritation, with gravelly deposits; difficult urination; bloody urine; deep-seated renal pain; hepatic pain; irritation of bronchial tract. It improves the nutrition of the urinary mucous tissues.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Hydrangea'', Hydrangea arborescens Linné, Nat. Ord. Saxifragaceae; sections History and Description (taste, seven barks), Chemical Composition (tannin), Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage, and Specific Indications and Uses. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/hydrangea.html (20 KB page downloaded whole and read in full)"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H252';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "女貞實　味苦，平。主補中，安五臟，養精神，除百疾。生山谷。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯), 卷一·上經, 木部上品, 女貞實 entry. Full text: https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=神農本草經_(孫星衍)&action=raw"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H275';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"Non-Official Kinds.—ALOE CAPENSIS (U. S. P., 1870), Cape aloes, Aloe lucida. Derived chiefly from Aloe spicata, Thunberg, Aloe ferox, Lamarck, and Aloe Lingua, Linné.\"\n\n\"ALOE CAPENSIS, Cape aloes (Shining aloes). Cape aloes has a glossy or resinous fracture, a deep-brown or olive color, with a greenish tint, a shining, smooth surface; and thin scales of it are nearly transparent, having a ruby color. Its odour is more powerful and unpleasant than the Barbadoes aloes; its taste peculiar and bitter; and its powder is bright yellow, somewhat like gamboge, but having a greenish tint.\"\n\n\"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Tonic, purgative, emmenagogue, and anthelmintic. ... As a laxative and purgative, its applications are unbounded; it acts more especially on the muscular coat of the large intestines, rather increasing their peristaltic motion than effecting copious, thin or watery discharges; and from its tendency to produce heat and irritation about the anus, it is extremely improper (except in minute doses in debilitated individuals) for persons disposed to or troubled with piles. ... some imagine it to influence the duodenum, especially the mouths of the biliary ducts, causing an increased flow of bile and stimulating the intestinal canal, when that secretion is suspended, as in jaundice. It acts upon the uterus, promoting the menstrual flow ...\"\n\n\"For habitual constipation, aloes should never be given in cathartic doses. Debility and marked intestinal torpor point to its selection.\"\n\n\"Aloes should never be given in inflammatory affections, in irritable, plethoric habits, in gastritis, enteritis, and, as a rule, where piles are present; nor to females liable to sudden uterine evacuations, nor during pregnancy.\"\n\n\"Specific Indications and Uses.—''Atony of large intestine and rectum, mucoid discharges, prolapsus ani, pruritis ani, ascaris vermicularis'' (Scudder, Dis. of Children, 1882). Difficulty in evacuating the lower bowel.\"", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Aloe\" (Nat. Ord. Liliaceae), sections Official/Non-Official Kinds; Description and Tests (ALOE CAPENSIS); Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage; Specific Indications and Uses. https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/aloe.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H299';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "The seeds are \"about 2 Cm. (4/5 inch) long, broadly-ovate, flat, white or whitish, nearly smooth, with a shallow groove parallel to the edge; containing a short, conical radicle, and 2 flat cotyledons; inodorous; taste bland and oily\"—(U. S. P.). ... Mucilaginous, taenicide, and diuretic, and of service in strangury and urinary affections, also in gastritis, enteritis, and febrile diseases. ... Specific Indications and Uses.—Tapeworm; roundworm; ardor urinae.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Pepo (U. S. P.)—Pumpkin Seed\", sections \"Description\" (taste sentence, quoted from U.S.P.), \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\", and \"Specific Indications and Uses\". Live page: https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/cucurbita-pepo.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H118';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "[rind, from ''Description and Chemical Composition'':] GRANATI FRUCTUS CORTEX. The rind of the fruit (Granati fructus cortex), when dry, is brown externally, yellow within, about a line in thickness, smooth or finely tuberculated, hard, dry, brittle, in irregular fragments, inodorous, and of a very astringent, somewhat bitter taste.\n\n[root/stem bark, quoting the U.S.P.:] \"…inodorous; taste astringent, very slightly bitter\"—(U. S. P.).\n\n[from ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage'':] The flowers and rind of the fruit are astringent and have been used for arresting chronic mucous discharges, passive hemorrhages, aphthous disorders of the mouth, night sweats, colliquative diarrhoea, etc. […] The rind has also been found serviceable in intermittent fever and tapeworm. The bark of the root possesses anthelmintic properties, and is chiefly serviceable in tapeworm. […] Dizziness, imperfect vision, sleepiness, or faintness, benumbing of the extremities, and occasionally convulsions have been produced by it.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry headed ''Granatum (U. S. P.)—Pomegranate.''; sections ''Description and Chemical Composition'' (sub-headings GRANATI FRUCTUS CORTEX and GRANATI RADICIS CORTEX) and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''. Full text at https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/punica.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H144';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "If lobelia be chewed, it gives rise to an acrid, prickling, and persistently pungent sensation in the throat and fauces, accompanied by slight nausea and a feeling of warmth and distension along the esophageal tract and in the stomach. … Lobelia is nauseant, emetic, expectorant, relaxant, antispasmodic, diaphoretic, sialagogue, sedative, and, secondarily, occasionally cathartic and diuretic and astringent.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry LOBELIA (U. S. P.)—LOBELIA, section \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\", first and second paragraphs. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/lobelia.html. The taste is corroborated in the same entry''s \"Description\" (U. S. P.): \"odor slight, irritating; taste mild, afterward burning and acrid\", and in \"History\": \"a strong, acrid, nauseous taste developed by chewing\"."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H171';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "It has an agreeable, aromatic odor, and a sweet, spicy taste. […] Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—A stimulant and carminative; used in cases of flatulency, flatulent colic of infants, and to remove nausea. Sometimes added to other medicines to improve their flavor, correct griping and other disagreeable effects.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), ''Anisum (U. S. P.)—Anise'', sections ''Description'' (taste, quoting U.S.P.) and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''; henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/pimpinella-anis.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H194';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "淫羊藿\n味辛，寒。主陽痿絕傷，莖中痛，利小便，益氣力，強志。一名剛前。生山谷。\n《吳普》曰：淫羊藿，神農、雷公：辛；李氏：小寒。堅骨（《御覽》）。\n《名醫》曰：生上山郡陽山。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍・孫馮翼輯校, 1799), 中經 / 中品, 草部, 淫羊藿 entry. Retrieved as raw wikitext from zh.wikisource.org/wiki/神農本草經_(孫星衍); the herb is also listed in the 中品 index line of the same recension, confirming it is a genuine Benjing entry."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H227';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "薏苡仁\n\n味甘，微寒。主筋急，拘攣不可屈神，風濕痹，下氣。久服，輕身、益氣。其根，下三虫。一名解蠡。生平澤及田野。", "locator": "Shennong Ben Cao Jing 神農本草經, Sun Xingyan 孫星衍 recension (1799), 卷一·上經, entry 薏苡仁, standing between 薯蕷 and 澤瀉 and listed in that juan''s table of contents under 草上品七十三種. Retrieved as raw wikitext from https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=%E7%A5%9E%E8%BE%B2%E6%9C%AC%E8%8D%89%E7%B6%93_(%E5%AD%AB%E6%98%9F%E8%A1%8D)&action=raw (242 KB, downloaded whole with curl and grepped locally, not WebFetched)"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H253';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"In sections of plano-convex, circular cakes, or in irregular, angular pieces, externally grayish-brown, or dull reddish-brown, internally whitish or yellowish, of a waxy luster; odor heavy, somewhat narcotic; taste bitter\"—(U. S. P.). ... It is, when employed at all, usually given as a calmative and hypnotic, and as a substitute for opium, to which it is to be preferred in many instances, on account of its freedom from unpleasant after-effects, as constipation, excitement of the brain, etc. ... It appears to be of use in insomnia, due to mental overwork. A syrup of lactucarium is of value in the cough of phthisis", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Lactucarium (U. S. P.)—Lactucarium\", sections \"Description\" (U.S.P. taste line) and \"Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage\". Companion entry \"Lactuca.—Lettuce\" gives the herb-level taste: \"a bitter, narcotic, and somewhat acid taste, and an odor approximating that of opium\". Live pages: https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/lactuca_lactucarium.html and .../lactuca.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H119';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "[King''s American Dispensatory 1898, Cichorium.—Chicory:] The root is quite bitter, and imparts its virtues to water. […] Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Tonic, diuretic, and laxative. The decoction, used freely, is said to have proved efficient in jaundice, engorgement of the liver, and other chronic visceral diseases, as well as in cutaneous eruptions, gout, hectic fever, etc. An ounce of the root to a pint of water forms a good decoction.\n\n[Culpeper, The Complete Herbal 1653, Succory, or Chicory:] Garden Succory, as it is more dry and less cold than Endive, so it opens more. An handful of the leaves, or roots boiled in wine or water, and a draught thereof drank fasting, drives forth choleric and phlegmatic humours, opens obstructions of the liver, gall and spleen; helps the yellow jaundice, the heat of the reins, and of the urine […] The wild Succory, as it is more bitter, so it is more strengthening to the stomach and liver.\n\n[Culpeper, same work, catalogue of roots:] _Cichorii._ Of Succory; cool and dry in the second degree, strengthens the liver and veins, it opens obstructions, stoppings in the liver and spleen, being boiled in white wine and the decoction drank.", "locator": "(1) King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry ''Cichorium.—Chicory.'' (root of Cichorium Intybus, Linné; common names Chicory, Succory, Wild succory); sections ''History.'' and ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.'' — https://www.henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/cichorium.html  (2) Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (1653), entry headed ''SUCCORY, OR CHICORY.'', paragraph ''Government and virtues''; and the Simples/''Of Roots'' catalogue, entry ''_Cichorii._''. Project Gutenberg ebook #49513."}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H145';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "Bethroot is astringent, tonic, and antiseptic; it has been employed successfully in hemoptysis, hematuria, menorrhagia, uterine hemorrhage, metrorrhagia, leucorrhoea, cough, asthma, and difficult breathing, and is said to have been much used by the Indian women to promote parturition. The astringent varieties of Trillium have been found useful in hemorrhages. […] Externally, the root, made into a poultice, is very useful in tumors, indolent or offensive ulcers, anthrax, buboes, stings of insects, and to restrain gangrene.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory (1898), ''Trillium.—Bethroot.'', section ''Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage''; henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/trillium.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H197';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "\"The rhizome and roots of Cypripedium pubescens, Swartz, and of Cypripedium parviflorum, Salisbury\"—(U. S. P.). […] The rhizomes and fibrous roots of these plants are the parts used in medicine. They should be gathered in autumn, cleansed from dirt, and carefully dried in the shade. They have a peculiar, slightly bitter, and rather nauseous taste, and a somewhat unpleasant odor. Alcohol, or boiling water, takes up their virtues, which, however, are impaired by boiling. […] Description.—\"…brittle, dark-brown, or orange-brown; fracture short, white; odor peculiar, heavy; taste sweetish, bitter, and somewhat pungent\"—(U. S. P.). […] Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage.—Tonic, stimulant, diaphoretic, and antispasmodic. Its chief value is as a nerve stimulant in atonic cases, improving both the circulation and nutrition of the nerve centers (Scudder). […] This root is valuable in all cases of nervous excitability or irritability unconnected with organic lesions, allaying the irritability, lessening any accompanying pain, producing a more calm and cheerful condition of the body and mind, and consequently favoring mental tranquillity, or sleep.", "locator": "King''s American Dispensatory, 1898, entry \"Cypripedium (U. S. P.)—Cypripedium\", sub-sections History / Description / Action, Medical Uses, and Dosage. henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/kings/cypripedium.html"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H255';
update public.herbs set primary_text_citation = primary_text_citation || '{"excerpt": "百合　味甘，平。主邪氣腹張，心痛，利大小便，補中益氣。生川谷。 ｜ 金匱要略: 百合病者，百脈一宗，悉致其病也。意欲食復不能食，常默默，欲臥不能臥，欲行不能行……口苦，小便赤……其脈微數。 ｜ 百合病，不經吐、下、發汗，病形如初者，百合地黃湯主之。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯), 卷二·中經, 草部中品, 百合 entry. AND 金匱要略, 百合病狐惑陰陽毒病脈證並治第三 (chapter 3), opening 論 and the 百合地黃湯 clause. https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=金匱要略&action=raw"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H277';
commit;
